ClassiCube for Debian / the PS3
===============================

This package is the game engine only. The textures, sounds and music are Mojang's and are not
part of it, so that the package can be distributed on its own. The game needs them, and gets
them in one of three ways. All files live in the per-user game directory

    ~/.local/share/classicube/        ($XDG_DATA_HOME/classicube)

which /usr/games/classicube creates and runs the game from.

1. The game's own download (needs internet, the launcher shows the size)
   Start ClassiCube. With no data present its launcher shows "Some required resources weren't
   found" with the size and a Yes/No question. Answer Yes. It fetches
     - the texture pack, http://static.classicube.net/default.zip, saved as
       ~/.local/share/classicube/texpacks/classicube.zip
     - the sounds and music from Mojang's resource server
       (https://resources.download.minecraft.net/), saved as audio/default2.zip (sounds, written
       by the game itself) and audio/*.ogg (music)
   and then shows the main menu. Nothing is written outside the user directory. The launcher is
   driven with mouse and keyboard.

2. Copy the files by hand (no internet on the machine)
   Take them from another ClassiCube install (its texpacks/ and audio/ directories, made by
   the launcher as in 1.) and put them in the same places:
     ~/.local/share/classicube/texpacks/classicube.zip
     ~/.local/share/classicube/audio/default2.zip     (this name on big-endian machines like
                                                       the PS3, default.zip elsewhere)
     ~/.local/share/classicube/audio/*.ogg            (calm1-3, hal1-4 and so on)
   Sounds made on a little-endian machine (audio/default.zip) are not usable on the PS3: let
   the launcher fetch them there, or copy default2.zip from a PS3 install. The files the
   launcher builds from an installed Minecraft Classic client are the same Mojang assets.
   With every file present the launcher goes straight to the main menu. The game also runs
   without sounds or music; the texture pack is required.

3. The classicube-data package (our own LAN repository only)
   In the PS3 project's apt repository, the ps3-licensed suite has classicube-data, which
   installs the same files under /usr/lib/classicube/. /usr/games/classicube then copies them
   into the user directory on first start. It is for personal / LAN use and is not distributed
   with the game. ps3-games-licensed pulls it in along with the other licensed game data:

       sudo apt install ps3-games-licensed
       (or just: sudo apt install classicube-data)

Starting over: delete ~/.local/share/classicube/texpacks and audio; the next start fetches (or
copies) them again. Options, maps and screenshots in the same directory are left alone.
